{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact head 845c7fd777549bcbbf18d3807655819952e5a07d. All 27 named artifacts reviewed, including the prior unchanged contract sources and the complete repair diff. npm ci --ignore-scripts and npm run typecheck pass in the detached checker worktree; all five examples compile.\n\nThe five findings in 45431cd9 are answered:\n- P1.1: EntryContent precedes hashing; new-lane/revert IDs are derived projections; operation IDs use sequences; notify outcomes are later entries; the checkpoint inside a Git commit omits that commit's SHA, with the SHA attested in a subsequent event. The worked construction is acyclic.\n- P1.2: explicit config-recovery lanes bypass hostile policy through claim, propose, admin review and land, while preserving actual config-only changed paths, direct current admin authority, signatures, schema, lease/generation fencing and sole-admin reservation rules.\n- P1.3: member/delegation/join/recovery authority cases and the redeem wire route permit browser, MCP and unjoined Worker onboarding.\n- P1.4: WorkspaceOp is public and credential-free; workspaceToken is a separate signed, current-holder/current-lease retrieval. MCP exposes the grant only after the same authorization. Revoked/removed/expired callers and secret-bearing outputs have explicit rules.\n- P2.1: mandatory global inputs now include tests, fixtures and build/test configuration, with the new-failing-test acceptance case.\n\nRemaining P1: enforce invitation custody at every join admission (docs/protocol.md R-ADM-3(c), R-CRED-3 and R-CRED-9; packages/contract/src/transports.ts RoomWire.submit and HttpRoutes /acts; roster.ts Invitation).\nThe join case checks unused/unexpired invitation, secret hash and unbound signing key, but never requires the invitation's custody to match the redemption path. /acts and RoomWire.submit also admit signed roster/join envelopes. Concrete trace: an admin issues a custody:\"room\" invitation for a member with role admin and session.kinds:[\"note\"]; its recipient generates an extractable private key and submits an ordinary signed join with that invitation's secret through /acts. Every listed R-ADM-3(c) condition passes. The key becomes a direct member key, so subsequent acts use case (a), bypassing the intended session delegation's kind/lifetime bounds and room custody.\nSpecify that custody:\"room\" invitations can be consumed only by the trusted room redemption operation using a key generated and held there; reject an externally supplied join against that invitation on /acts, RPC submit and client redemption. Conversely room redemption must not consume a client-custody invitation. Route/body claims alone are not proof: the trusted internal admission context must enforce this, and it must not be a caller-controlled signed-envelope field. Keep signature/invitation checks, single consumption and ordinary client-custody self-signed joins. Add the cross-custody negative cases to the normative acceptance table, alongside the successful bounded MCP redemption case. This is a protocol/type boundary repair, not a request to implement Lane E early.\n\nArchitecture: the repaired log and recovery workflow are implementable without hash fixed points or a policy-controlled repair gate. Separate signed admissions, public operation projections and holder-only grant retrieval are sound. The remaining join loophole would undermine the shared authority model on every transport.\nSecurity: changes requested for the custody bypass above. Historical evidence and uncertain publication protections remain intact. A contract-level rule trace is the evidence here; no live Room exploit is claimed.\nSimplification: keep the four authority cases and one shared join admission implementation; add a narrow trusted custody condition rather than transport-specific authority variants or a new caller-controlled privilege flag. The explicit worked examples and acceptance cases improve handoff clarity.\n\nImplementation follow-ups that do not add approval conditions to this types-only delivery: qualify the general 'runtime failure records nothing' wording (R-ADM-9/R-EVAL-5) with the documented post-commit notify exception in R-LOG-13; Lane E should exercise refused/partial/lost-response redemption without leaking a bearer or silently consuming an invitation with no usable recovery path. Runtime log verification and credential confinement still need their specified acceptance tests in their implementation lanes. Full functional scope is preserved; estimates impose no cuts.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e7cbaa0ae86c0048a9654055c7377bdb971c9f42","binding":"assigned","head":"845c7fd777549bcbbf18d3807655819952e5a07d","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0ae0693a20cc24ad168cab44902643847b7bfb1c\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a121cd85bf737bc30126316df28adf092ebacdc1\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:20cf77e0d8e8478ea9044953ed5903b698055dc1","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}